  "notes": "The lane's most virtuosic modern voice, gospel-trained and working with jazz rhythm sections. The kit is the Chris Dave school taken to its limit: \"a displaced, Chris Dave-school live kit — snare placed a 32nd off 2&4, ghost strokes filling the gaps, hats crushed or absent, plus long drumless passages and free-time intros. Programmed drums are rare.\" So this model carries the largest snare displacement in the section (14–28 ms), a 0.72 ghost probability across all eight 16th positions, a non-continuous hat at a fill density of 0.26, and the lowest `quantizeStrength` here at 0.36. Harmony is \"gospel-jazz — dense extensions (m11, maj9#11, altered dominants), reharmonisation on every repeat, chromatic inner-voice motion, and modulation mid-song. Flat keys dominate\", which is why no families are authored (reharmonisation on every repeat is the opposite of a fixed family), the triad weight is 0.02 and `susSubstitutionProb` is the highest in this batch. The melodic claim is the entry's load-bearing one: \"improvisatory melisma — very high note-density, long runs across a two-octave-plus range, riffing over the harmony rather than stating a fixed line, and a whisper-to-full-belt dynamic span within a single phrase. She rarely repeats a melodic figure identically.\" That is density 6–13, a three-octave register, `endVariation`, a high octave-jump probability and the lowest `chordToneBias` in this batch — riffing over the harmony, not landing on it. Bass is \"played electric or upright with a soloistic, chord-tone-heavy role and wide register\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody figures; the research marks tempo and harmonic detail as inference and [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ `session.keys` is INFERRED too: the entry's Keys line names no key at all and ends only \"Flat keys dominate.\", so the five values are this author's flat-side reading of that single constraint rather than a list the research gives.",
